      "timestamp": "2026-11-27T03:25:16.650880+00:00",
      "source": "user",
      "message": "You are computer-1, an autonomous agent that controls a desktop computer to\ncomplete tasks. Each turn you observe the current screen via a screenshot and\nrespond with one action.\n\nTask instructions:\nGUI-only evaluation. Work in the Blender desktop using mouse and keyboard, observing screenshots. Do not open a terminal, Python Console, Scripting workspace, execute scripts or use command injection. You have no shell tool. Do not type code into Blender, a terminal, search box or editor to execute it. Use the task's native GUI operations to build from scratch, save the named .blend, and render/save outputs. You may type JSON into a plain text editor to save manifest.json; do not execute it. Use /app/materials and /app/material-previews (faithful PDF/table views of the supplied materials). Task reference solutions, grading scripts and prior runs are unavailable. Finish the work autonomously. Save progress frequently in /app/out. If the time limit arrives, preserve your actual partial output; do not claim missing deliverables exist.\n\nYou are working in a Blender 4.2 desktop session. `/app/materials` holds the\ndesign pack for a folding chair; `/app/out` is where you deliver.\n\n## What you are given\n\n- `materials/drawings/hinge_layout.png` (and `.pdf`) \u2014 side elevation with the\n  four hinge positions in millimetres.\n- `materials/drawings/states.pdf` \u2014 the four states and what makes each one\n  observable.\n- `materials/tables/chair_sizes.xlsx` \u2014 seat height 450, width 620, depth 580,\n  height 820, folded thickness 90.\n- `materials/tables/hinge_layout.xlsx` \u2014 hinge coordinates and range.\n- `materials/photos/` \u2014 unfolded, half, folded, locked and a hinge close-up.\n\n## What to build\n\nModel the chair in metres, at millimetres from the drawing.\n\n1. Parts: `seat`, `back`, `front_leg_l`, `front_leg_r`, `rear_leg_l`,\n   `rear_leg_r`, and four hinges `hinge_01` \u2026 `hinge_04` at the coordinates in\n   the table.\n2. The two legs on each side form a scissor pinned through `hinge_01` /\n   `hinge_02`; the seat pivots on `hinge_03` and the back on `hinge_04`. Bars\n   are parented to the hinge empties, so the joint stays coincident when the\n   hinge rotates. Do not use shape keys or baked vertex animation.\n3. Unfolded (frame 0): 620 \u00d7 580 \u00d7 820 mm, seat top at 450 mm. Folded (frame\n   60): a 90 mm pack. Animate 0\u201360 at 24 fps with the hinges as the drivers.\n4. No part may pass through another at any frame: sample the fold every two\n   frames. Contact at a pinned joint is fine; penetration elsewhere is not.\n5. At most 120k triangles. Textures packed.\n\n## What to deliver in /app/out\n\n| File | Camera | Resolution |\n|---|---|---|\n| `folding_chair.blend` | \u2014 | \u2014 |\n| `render_unfolded.png` | `cam_states`, frame 0 | 1200 \u00d7 1200 |\n| `render_half.png` | `cam_states`, frame 20 | 1200 \u00d7 1200 |\n| `render_folded.png` | `cam_states`, frame 60 | 1200 \u00d7 1200 |\n| `render_locked.png` | `cam_states`, frame 0 with the lock pin visible | 1200 \u00d7 1200 |\n| `manifest.json` | \u2014 | \u2014 |\n\nCycles CPU, 64 samples, denoise on, fixed seed. The camera stays in the file as\n`cam_states`; the four renders share it.\n\n`manifest.json` lists every asset with a source and a licence, e.g.\n\n```json\n{\"schemaVersion\": 1, \"units\": \"meters\", \"blenderVersion\": \"4.2.3\",\n \"assets\": [{\"path\": \"textures/oak_basecolor.png\", \"role\": \"texture\",\n             \"source\": \"self-authored\", \"license\": \"CC0-1.0\", \"url\": \"\"}],\n \"renders\": [{\"file\": \"render_unfolded.png\", \"camera\": \"cam_states\",\n              \"resolution\": [1200, 1200], \"samples\": 64}]}\n```\n\n## How it is checked\n\nThe file must reopen; all ten parts/hinges exist; hinges within 2 mm of the\nlayout; unfolded size 620 \u00d7 580 \u00d7 820 \u00b15 mm; folded pack \u2264 95 mm; 0\u201360 frames\nat 24 fps; no interpenetration across 31 sampled poses; each leg's centroid\nwithin 20 mm of its hinge axis; four 1200 \u00d7 1200 renders from `cam_states`;\ntextures packed; manifest complete.\n\nAnti-cheat: the scene is re-rendered at frames 0 and 30 from `cam_states`. The\ndelivered unfolded render must match the frame-0 re-render (SSIM \u2265 0.90) and\nframe 30 must differ from frame 0 (SSIM \u2264 0.98).\n\nEach delivered view/state is independently re-rendered from its specified\ncamera. It must match in every RGB channel (SSIM >= 0.90) and in image\nstructure (relative RGB gradient error after a 2-pixel Gaussian blur <= 0.60).\nAgreement on a large uniform background alone is insufficient. Compositing\nor a video sequence cannot substitute for the delivered 3D scene.\n\nYou have 7200 seconds to complete this task.\n\n\nYou interact with the computer through a private runtime. On every turn you\nwill see a fresh screenshot of the current desktop. The display is\n1920x1080 pixels. All click/move/scroll/drag\ncoordinates you produce MUST be in raw desktop pixels (no normalization).\n\nInitial screen state:\nsee attached screenshot.\n\nResponse format\n===============\n\nRespond with EXACTLY one JSON object and nothing else (no surrounding prose,\nno Markdown fences). The object must validate against this shape:\n\n{\n  \"analysis\": \"<short notes about the screen / progress>\",\n  \"plan\": \"<your plan for the next few steps>\",\n  \"action\": {\n    \"type\": \"<one of: click, double_click, triple_click, right_click, mouse_down, mouse_up, mouse_move, type, keypress, hold_key, scroll, drag, zoom, navigate, wait, done, answer>\",\n    \"x\": <int, optional>,\n    \"y\": <int, optional>,\n    \"end_x\": <int, optional, only for drag>,\n    \"end_y\": <int, optional, only for drag>,\n    \"text\": <string, optional, used by type and answer>,\n    \"keys\": <list of strings, optional, used by keypress and hold_key>,\n    \"url\": <string, optional, used by navigate>,\n    \"scroll_x\": <int, optional, used by scroll>,\n    \"scroll_y\": <int, optional, used by scroll>,\n    \"button\": <\"left\"|\"middle\"|\"right\", optional, used by click>,\n    \"modifier\": <\"shift\"|\"ctrl\"|\"alt\"|\"super\", optional, held during click/double_click/triple_click/right_click/scroll>,\n    \"duration\": <number of seconds, optional, used by hold_key>,\n    \"zoom_region\": <[x0, y0, x1, y1] in desktop pixels, optional, used by zoom>,\n    \"result\": <string, optional, the final answer for done/answer>\n  }\n}\n\nRules\n=====\n\n- Output exactly ONE action per turn. Do not batch.\n- For \"click\", \"double_click\", \"triple_click\", \"right_click\", \"mouse_move\",\n  \"mouse_down\", \"mouse_up\", \"scroll\", \"drag\": provide raw desktop pixel\n  coordinates in \"x\"/\"y\" (and \"end_x\"/\"end_y\" for drag).\n- For \"type\": provide the literal text in \"text\". The text is sent to the\n  currently focused field.\n- For \"keypress\": provide a list of key names in \"keys\" (e.g. [\"ctrl\", \"l\"]).\n- For \"hold_key\": provide \"keys\" plus \"duration\" in seconds. The keys are\n  pressed, held for \"duration\" (default 1s), then released.\n- For \"scroll\": provide \"scroll_y\" in pixels (positive=down, negative=up) and\n  optionally \"scroll_x\" (positive=right, negative=left). Pass \"modifier\" to\n  hold a key (e.g. \"ctrl\" for zoom-scroll).\n- For click variants and scroll, set \"modifier\" to one of \"shift\"/\"ctrl\"/\n  \"alt\"/\"super\" to hold that key for the duration of the action.\n- For \"zoom\": provide \"zoom_region\" as [x0, y0, x1, y1] in desktop pixels.\n  The NEXT screenshot is cropped (no resize) to that region, then auto-resets.\n  Use this to inspect a small UI area at native pixel density.\n- For \"navigate\": provide the destination URL in \"url\".\n- For \"wait\": no fields are required; the runtime will pause briefly.\n- When you have completed the task, emit a \"done\" or \"answer\" action with the\n  final answer in \"result\". The harness writes \"result\" to\n  /logs/agent/final_answer.txt for the verifier.\n\nOutput the JSON object now.\n"
    },
